10c7|n|The act of impelling, or driving onward with sudden force; impulsion; especially, force so communicated as to produced motion suddenly, or immediately. ; The effect of an impelling force; motion produced by a sudden or momentary force. ; The action of a force during a very small interval of time; the effect of such action; as, the impulse of a sudden blow upon a hard elastic body. ; A mental force which simply and directly urges to action; hasty inclination; sudden motive; momentary or transient influence of appetite or passion; propension; incitement; as, a man of good impulses; passion often gives a violent impulse to the will.	a:1z59,62,4;1cax,62,4;nd6,59,7;1vwu,49,9;dyb,44,2;1bxz,33,2;11r2,30,3;22vf,17,2;1649,17,3;1wir,14,5;1ces,10,3;rwe,8,9;16xy,7,2;1uus,6,2~rwe,8,9;1vwu,49,9;nd6,59,7;1wir,14,5;1z59,62,4;1cax,62,4;1649,17,3;11r2,30,3;1ces,10,3;1uus,6,2;1bxz,33,2;22vf,17,2;16xy,7,2;dyb,44,2	V:1cp0,18,1~1cp0,18,1	n:130a,84,3;1cam,80,8;d8r,36,3;q8x,23,3;oq5,19,2;1vwu,18,1~1cam,80,8;q8x,23,3;d8r,36,3;130a,84,3;oq5,19,2;1vwu,18,1	N:239s,45,2;21tb,19,1;1lx8,17,2;fdl,4,2~fdl,4,2;1lx8,17,2;239s,45,2;21tb,19,1
